<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samogitia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Žemaitija</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samogitia is a historical region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> and the Baltic coast. Its people resisted the Teutonic and Livonian orders for more than a century, and the land, which separated the orders' Prussian and Livonian territories, was repeatedly ceded and recovered by Lithuania. It was confirmed as Lithuanian by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno">Peace of Melno</a> in 1422 and was among the last regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to be Christianised, from 1413.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samogitia.jpg" alt="Samogitia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Augustas Didžgalvis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palanga_by_Augustas_Didzgalvis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
